Explanation of shell model computation of friction of objects
by extrapolation of Seidel computations to zero bead size.

1.  Several programs place beads on a shell.  They are:
	ELIPSE.F4  places beads centered on ellipsoid surface
	SPHRIP.F4  places beads inside tangent to sphere surface
	CYLNDR.F4  places beads on a cylinder
	SHELLS.F4  generates multisphere object from set of beads on
a sphere and the coords of centers of multisphere object.  Example:
	.R F4
	*SPHRIP/G
	*TEMP.DA</7$
	TYPE A,R (2F7.0): 20,2.7
(generates shell of inside tangent beads on a sphere)
	.R F4
	*SHELLS/G
	*TEMP.DA/5
	*TETRAH.DA/6   (tetrah., a=20.)
	*TETR1.DA</7$
(generates shell of tetrahedral tetramer)
	.ED TETR1.DA   (to fix title)

2.  Seidel computations.  SEIDEL.F4 handles up to 640 beads; SEIDL2.F4
up to 1650 beads, using disk storage of force vector.  Optimized load 
modules exist as SEIDEL.LD and SEIDL2.LD.  Coordinates on unit 5
(I5,3F10.5), interruption files on units 6 (previous) and 7 (new).

3.  Extrapolations to zero bead size.  This is done by GENLSQ to get
quadratic least squares polynomial.  Set SSW0 to get printout of 
coefficient matrix (C) and inverse (C').  For estimate of standard error
of intercept, estimate the uncertainty R of the Seidel results, calculate
SQRT(SIGMA(R(I))/N) for N Seidel computations, multiply by SQRT(C'00).
This is the independent estimate of the standard error of the intercept,
and will likely be greater than the +/- figure calculated by GENLSQ.
In any case the larger figure must be used.
